.TH DHCPC 1M "31 October 2026" "dhcp-client"
.SH NAME
dhcpc \- get and keep a network address from a DHCP server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dhcpc
[
.B \-b
.I seconds
]
.I interface
.br
.B dhcpc \-x
.I interface
.br
.B dhcpc \-q
.I interface
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dhcpc
asks a DHCP server for an address for
.I interface
and keeps it: one
.B dhcpc
runs in the background for each interface that gets its address this way.
It is normally started by
.BR ifup (1M)
for an interface whose line in
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
says
.BR dhcp ,
and stopped by
.BR ifdown .
.PP
At start it first asks again for the address it held last, if that lease
has not ended; otherwise it looks for a server.
Once the lease is bound,
.B dhcpc
renews it with the same server halfway through, and with any server near
its end; when it ends anyway, or a server refuses it, the address is taken
off and
.B dhcpc
starts looking again, and goes on trying for as long as it runs.
.PP
.B dhcpc
changes nothing itself: every address, route, host name and name-server
change is made by
.BR /usr/lib/inet/dhcpc-hook ,
the same steps
.B ifup
uses for a static line, so a DHCP interface and a static one are set up
alike.
The machine's name is taken from the server only when
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
says
.BR "hostname dhcp" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-b " seconds"
wait at most this long for the first lease before returning; without it,
wait until there is one.
.TP
.B \-x
give the lease back to the server, take the address off, and stop the
.B dhcpc
of
.IR interface .
.TP
.B \-q
print the lease held for
.I interface
as
.IB name = value
lines.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
a lease is bound and applied (with
.BR \-q :
a lease is held now).
.TP
1
the interface cannot do DHCP (no driver for it, or it does not answer as
DLPI requires), or another
.B dhcpc
already holds it.
.TP
2
no lease within
.I seconds
yet; the background
.B dhcpc
goes on trying.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /var/inet/dhcpc/ interface .lease
the lease held, in the hook's
.B DHCP_
names.
.TP
.BI /var/inet/dhcpc/ interface .pid
the background
.BR dhcpc .
.TP
.B /var/adm/dhcpc.log
what each
.B dhcpc
did.
.TP
.B /etc/inet/link.d/*
where the interface's device is found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifup (1M),
.BR interfaces (4)
.SH NOTES
.B dhcpc
sends no client identifier; servers know it by its station address.
A renewal near the end of a lease is sent as a broadcast on the local
network only, so a server reached through a relay agent cannot extend a
lease that its first server stopped answering for.
